joining us now is the science minister  george freeman mr freeman good to talk  to you this morning thank you for  joining us we were promised a plan by  your colleagues rowlite plan uh earlier  on this week where's the plan  morning kay yeah good to join you um  today we've got all of the global fire  pharma leaders here in london to  announce another half a billion pound  package  into uk life science and as your report  highlights this pandemic isn't over and  the uk is leading in both the science of  defeating this virus  and in the rollout package for vaccines  here in the uk we've procured another  114 million doses so we've got the doses  here for patients over the next few  months and we're in a global race to  make sure we defeat this pandemic and  i'm here today to talk about how uk  science is right on the front line of  that we're working across government to  lead the global fight to defeat the  virus  and an answer to my  question yeah so we're we're working  right now across government on how we  both uh accelerate the rollout  internationally of vaccines the truth is  we're not safe until the world is safe  and the uk has led that charge we've  invested more per capita than any other  nation we're huge supporters of kovacs  and at the g7 summit  this summer we led the charge on how  globally we support international  rollout here in the uk our vaccine  rollout program is setting the benchmark  and that's why we're able this year to  not impose  the very tough uh quite draconian  regulations we had to last year  in a way that other countries are so  this is uk science leadership and nhs  leadership and the vaccine rollout  program  leading  here in the uk in terms of science but  also allowing our citizens to enjoy  fewer restrictions than in other  countries  on monday we were promised a plan it's  thursday we haven't got a plan people  don't know when they can have their  boosters where's the plan  the nhs i'll  have a plan it's being rolled out and  people can apply now i've just had my  text last night my booster appointment  uh has been offered for next week and  people across my constituency and around  the country are able now to get their  boosters and i would having had covered  myself three weeks ago this is not a  nice disease i was double jabbed went  down for a week and i would encourage  everybody  contact your gp get on the app  book your booster jab the nhs is  there we're rolling it out we've  procured the vaccines and we're leading  the world in that rollout so this is  good news i just encourage everyone  to make sure we get there  yeah in your 20s and 30s when can you go  onto the app to book your job  so if you contact your gp there are  slight differences around the country um  but at the moment we're the first  country to get to this point we've got  eighty percent of the whole country  double jabbed uh and uh we've got uh  nearly uh ninety percent of the whole  country first jabbed and we're now  rolling it out fast now some gp  practices are rolling slightly quicker  than others  and that's what you'd expect but uh if  people contact their gp now and go on  the app they can book uh to get their  their booster jabbing or their second  channel that's not true minister you  can't get your booster at the moment if  you're in your twenties the question is  we were told earlier in the week that we  wouldn't there's a plan i'm just asking  you where the plan is and when people in  their 20s and 30s can book their booster  well the nhs are leading on that role  that i haven't got on my fingertips  today exactly which gp practices are  making it available today but we are  accelerating that rollout and it is key  uh and we were we are uh putting that in  place so that the next generation uh the  next age cohort can get uh their booster  jab as quickly as possible but uh the  nhs has got uh to put that alongside  clearing the back club of cancer cases  and all of the other issues that the  pandemic has caused i think the public  understand that we have to do everything  we can we are the nhs is flat out i was  in constituency surgeries in my patch  last week and the booster rollout and  the jab program is working i'm not  getting a lot of complaints from people  saying the uk is behind on its  vaccination program of course we all  need to move fast and we are and the nhs  is doing that that's why we've put more  money in we only have a limited minister  we only have a limited amount of time  the answer is you don't know the answer  let me let me put this to you south  africa averaging 300 cases no you don't  the answer if you're only 20.  if you're in your 20s you don't know  when you can have the job just just  admit to that because that's that is  fundamentally the case isn't it  the case is that the nhs is rolling out  the vaccine program and if you contact  your gp and book it you will get your  your your jab it's not true it's just  not true it's just not true minister um  south africa um averaging 300 cases a  day that has led to eight and a half  thousand cases yesterday how concerned  are we  yeah well as the prime minister and the  health secretary child secretary have  set out we are concerned that's why  we've put in place  uh some pretty important reasonable  measures to make sure that red list  countries have to test before they come  we're trying to make sure that the  global economy can carry on working  while minimizing the risk and i think  that's the key we in those countries  where  the new variant is surging we need to be  quite tough and make sure that people  aren't bringing it in contain it  and then when it's here make sure that  we're containing it here in the uk we  did it before we've learned a huge  amount in the last year and we'll do it  again and today  we've got all the global leaders in the  vaccine industry here because the uk is  leading the charge globally this is a  pandemic it isn't over yet and the uk is  leading in making sure that we both  tackle the virus  and make sure that we're leading in the  rollout program  given that how many doses have we  delivered to kovacs we promised 100  million didn't we  yeah the problem with kovacs actually  has been and i set a up a commission  last year before coming back into  government that the global manufacturing  supply chains for example india a major  manufacturer of vaccines around the  world india was hit really hard by the  pandemic and one of the things we set  out in our g7 summit in the summer which  is partly what we're picking up today is  to make sure that we've got those global  supply chains so britain has put the  funding in we've procured the vaccines  the key is getting the roll out  internationally into some of those  hard-to-reach countries in africa in the  some of the pacific island states and  that is a global challenge that's why  we're one of the biggest contributors to  kovacs who are doing a brilliant job  we've put the vaccines in we've got to  make sure that we support them in  rolling them out internationally because  as i say  we're not safe the global economy isn't  safe until we've defeated this pandemic  across the whole world  we promised a hundred million how many  have we delivered  we've we've supported that 100 million  they're in the pipeline that isn't the  issue okay the issue is getting them out  around the country which is why we're  focusing today on  how many of we've delivered  i'm sorry i'm sorry how many have we  delivered of the 100 million that we  promised you mean have we  have we delivered to kovacs  yes  yeah we we're on schedule i haven't got  that number at my fingertips now but we  are  on schedule the issue isn't lack of you  yeah that but that isn't what's holding  up the global rollout program okay  what's holding it up as i say is that  international  supply chain issue around the world and  nobody is saying we're not getting them  enough because the uk hasn't supported  so why 