  changes we're guided by the science and  we stand ready as partly why we've  procured the vaccines to make sure that  we can deliver what our citizens and  patients need  okay we are out of town i just want to  ask you one final question before i let  you go though minister if you will  indulge me apparently emmanuel macron  has uh said it's very sad to see a great  country with which we could do so much  led by a clown  uh talking about boris johnson what  would you say in response  well i think we're into pantomime season  aren't we and there's a french election  coming it's a pretty unhelpful word of  course the prime minister's in the clown  he's the elected prime minister of this  country with a very big mandate leading  this country through the pandemic the  truth is we are looking to work very  closely with france on the border issue  on tackling the problem of european  migration at source that's why we're  investing in trying to stabilize  countries so that people aren't coming  here and with france we need to make  sure that people in fro in france aren't  being supplied with boats and being  pushed out into the channel the home  secretary is working closely with french  counterparts on it and the prime  minister and the uk government is  looking for a sensible conversation with  france about it so uh i i'm confident  that actually anglo-french relations are  rather better than that quote suggests  are you planning a lovely christmas  well  i think all of us are hoping that this  christmas we'll be able to enjoy  more freedom than we did last christmas  um  but uh you know we're in this pandemic  and hopefully this new variant isn't  going to sweep through  the country and we won't have to put in  place those restrictions last year and  the vaccine program is our best defense  against that um so i think everyone  as the prime minister said should be  able to plan for christmas but we're  going to have to be sensible and that's  why we've upped the guidance  masks on public transport  masks in shops but we also want people  to be able to go to  clubs and to pubs and be able to have a  drink  and eat which you can't do if you're  wearing a mask it's common sense really  i think the british public understand  that the more we can prevent the  transmission of the virus the more we'll  be able to enjoy the freedoms we all  cherish and  to me wearing a mask on a tube in winter  weather particularly isn't onerous and i  think if we wear masks in shops and on  public transport we've got a higher  chance of being able to enjoy those  freedoms we all cherish  okay good to talk to you minister thank  you so much for taking the time much  appreciated  thank you

